# Line endings are part of this package's contract, not a matter of taste.
#
# Version 0.1.0 shipped 97 CRLF files inside the npm tarball. This file is the fix.
#
# What happened: the repository was published from a Windows checkout, where
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the default. Git rewrote every LF file to CRLF on
# checkout, and `npm publish` packs the working tree rather than the stored blobs.
# So the tarball carried CRLF even though the repository was stored as LF.
#
# What that broke, for every person who ran the installer:
#
#   - `npm run verify` in a scaffolded frontend failed on every generated file,
#     because prettier's default `endOfLine: "lf"` rejects CRLF. The scaffolded
#     module could not pass the check its own README tells it to run.
#   - Spotless and Checkstyle read the same CRLF sources, so the backend gate had
#     a second, independent way to fail.
#
# The generated repositories are JavaScript, JSON, YAML, XML and Markdown. A
# formatter governs every one of them, so LF is the only line ending that can be
# guaranteed to survive `mvn verify` and `npm run verify` on every contributor's
# machine regardless of their operating system.

# Normalise everything to LF in the working tree, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The generated backend is Spring Boot: its BOM and every Spring artifact are LF,
# and a CRLF-patched .mvn/ directory has produced corrupted class files in the
# wild. Never let a checkout touch it.
.mvn/** text eol=lf
*.mvn text eol=lf

# Binary-ish files: leave them exactly as they are. `text=auto` would otherwise try
# to diff them as text and a stray byte would corrupt them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.jar binary
*.class binary
